ls after a head injury mean go to the ER now. Know the red flags, plus risks for blood thinner users.\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"10\",{\"property\":\"og:url\",\"content\":\"https://www.gale.care/health/head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"11\",{\"property\":\"og:type\",\"content\":\"article\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"12\",{\"property\":\"article:published_time\",\"content\":\"2026-06-12\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"13\",{\"property\":\"article:modified_time\",\"content\":\"2026-07-10\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"14\",{\"name\":\"twitter:card\",\"content\":\"summary_large_image\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"15\",{\"name\":\"twitter:title\",\"content\":\"Head Injury: When to Go to the ER — Key Warning Signs · Gale\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"16\",{\"name\":\"twitter:description\",\"content\":\"Confusion, repeated vomiting, seizures, or unequal pupils after a head injury mean go to the ER now. Know the red flags, plus risks for blood thinner users.\"}],[\"$\",\"link\",\"17\",{\"rel\":\"icon\",\"href\":\"/icon.png?icon.2px5wycl5x6on.png\",\"sizes\":\"256x256\",\"type\":\"image/png\"}],[\"$\",\"link\",\"18\",{\"rel\":\"apple-touch-icon\",\"href\":\"/apple-icon.png?apple-icon.44b3vq2f5400v.png\",\"sizes\":\"180x180\",\"type\":\"image/png\"}],[\"$\",\"$L33\",\"19\",{}]]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"34:I[522016,[\"$10\",\"$11\",\"$12\",\"$13\",\"$14\",\"$18\",\"$19\",\"$1a\",\"$1b\",\"$1c\",\"$1d\",\"$1e\",\"$1f\"],\"\"]\n35:I[605500,[\"$10\",\"$11\",\"$12\",\"$13\",\"$14\",\"$18\",\"$19\",\"$1a\",\"$1b\",\"$1c\",\"$1d\",\"$1e\",\"$1f\"],\"Image\"]\n36:I[811058,[\"$10\",\"$11\",\"$12\",\"$13\",\"$14\",\"$18\",\"$19\",\"$1a\",\"$1b\",\"$1c\",\"$1d\",\"$1e\",\"$1f\"],\"FontSizeMenu\"]\n37:I[332617,[\"$10\",\"$11\",\"$12\",\"$13\",\"$14\",\"$18\",\"$19\",\"$1a\",\"$1b\",\"$1c\",\"$1d\",\"$1e\",\"$1f\"],\"PublicNavLinks\"]\n38:I[43539,[\"$10\",\"$11\",\"$12\",\"$13\",\"$14\",\"$18\",\"$19\",\"$1a\",\"$1b\",\"$1c\",\"$1d\",\"$1e\",\"$1f\"],\"ArrivalBeacon\"]\n39:Tec6,{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@graph\":[{\"@type\":[\"MedicalWebPage\",\"Article\"],\"@id\":\"https://www.gale.care/health/head-injury-when-to-go-to-er#article\",\"url\":\"https://www.gale.care/health/head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\",\"mainEntityOfPage\":\"https://www.gale.care/health/head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\",\"headline\":\"Head Injury: Warning Signs That Mean Go to the ER Now\",\"description\":\"Confusion, repeated vomiting, seizures, or unequal pupils after a head injury mean go to the ER now. Know the red flags, plus risks for blood thinner users.\",\"datePublished\":\"2026-06-12\",\"dateModified\":\"2026-07-10\",\"inLanguage\":\"en-US\",\"isPartOf\":{\"@id\":\"https://www.gale.care/#website\"},\"author\":{\"@id\":\"https://www.gale.care/#organization\"},\"publisher\":{\"@id\":\"https://www.gale.care/#organization\"},\"about\":{\"@type\":\"MedicalCondition\",\"name\":\"head injury when to go to the er\"},\"audience\":{\"@type\":\"MedicalAudience\",\"audienceType\":\"Patient\"},\"mainEntity\":[{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Can I have a serious head injury without losing consciousness?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Yes. A concussion does not require loss of consciousness. Someone can feel fine immediately after a head impact and develop symptoms — headache, fogginess, nausea — minutes to hours later. If any of the red flag symptoms appear after a head injury, go to the ER regardless of whether consciousness was lost.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Is it safe to sleep after a head injury?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"For most minor bumps with no red flag symptoms, sleep is safe. The old advice to keep someone awake all night is not current guidance. However, it is reasonable to check on the person periodically to make sure they can be roused and are not confused. If they become very difficult to wake or develop new symptoms, go to the ER immediately.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Why are blood thinners a special concern after a head injury?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Blood thinners reduce the blood's ability to clot. After a head injury, a small bleed that a healthy person's body would stop can continue to expand in someone on anticoagulants. This can cause pressure on the brain even from an injury that seemed minor. Any head injury in someone on warfarin, apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, or higher-dose aspirin warrants emergency evaluation.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"When can someone with a concussion return to sport or activity?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Return to sport or strenuous activity after a concussion should follow a stepwise protocol guided by a clinician, with each stage completed symptom-free before advancing. Returning too quickly — while still having any symptoms — raises the risk of a second injury before the brain has healed. A sports medicine or primary care clinician can help build this plan.\"}}],\"citation\":[{\"@type\":\"CreativeWork\",\"name\":\"Signs and Symptoms of Concussion\",\"url\":\"https://www.cdc.gov/heads-up/signs-symptoms/index.html\"},{\"@type\":\"CreativeWork\",\"name\":\"Head Injury in Children: How to Know If It's Minor or Serious\",\"url\":\"https://www.healthychildren.org/English/health-issues/injuries-emergencies/Pages/Head-Injury.aspx\"},{\"@type\":\"CreativeWork\",\"name\":\"ACR Appropriateness Criteria® Head Trauma: 2021 Update\",\"url\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33958108/\"}]},{\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"Health library\",\"item\":\"https://www.gale.care/health\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Urgent \u0026 emergency\",\"item\":\"https://www.gale.care/health/topics/crisis\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":3,\"name\":\"Head Injury: Warning Signs That Mean Go to the ER Now\",\"item\":\"https://www.gale.care/health/head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\"}]}]}29:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"min-h-screen overflow-x-clip\",\"children\":[[[\"$\",\"nav\",null,{\"className\":\"rule-b\",\"style\":{\"background\":\"rgba(248,244,242,.85)\"},\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mx-auto flex max-w-6xl items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-2.5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/\",\"aria-label\":\"Gale — home\",\"className\":\"inline-flex items-center\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L35\",null,{\"src\":\"/gale-bird.png\",\"alt\":\"Gale\",\"width\":40,\"height\":40,\"priority\":true}]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex items-center gap-4\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L36\",null,{}],[\"$\",\"$L37\",null,{\"cta\":false,\"plain\":false,\"entry\":true}]]}]]}]}],[\"$\",\"$L38\",null,{}],null],[\"$\",\"article\",null,{\"className\":\"px-6 pb-20 pt-8\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"script\",null,{\"type\":\"application/ld+json\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"$39\"}}],\"$L3a\",\"$L3b\",\"$L3c\",\"$L3d\",\"$L3e\",\"$L3f\",\"$L40\",\"$L41\",false,\"$L42\"]}],\"$L43\"]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"44:I[368671,[\"$10\",\"$11\",\"$12\",\"$13\",\"$14\",\"$18\",\"$19\",\"$1a\",\"$1b\",\"$1c\",\"$1d\",\"$1e\",\"$1f\"],\"TrailRecorder\"]\n45:\"ArticleViewBeacon\"\n46:I[436900,[\"$10\",\"$11\",\"$12\",\"$13\",\"$14\",\"$18\",\"$19\",\"$1a\",\"$1b\",\"$1c\",\"$1d\",\"$1e\",\"$1f\"],\"$45\"]\n47:I[436900,[\"$10\",\"$11\",\"$12\",\"$13\",\"$14\",\"$18\",\"$19\",\"$1a\",\"$1b\",\"$1c\",\"$1d\",\"$1e\",\"$1f\"],\"ReadingBeacon\"]\n48:I[391465,[\"$10\",\"$11\",\"$12\",\"$13\",\"$14\",\"$18\",\"$19\",\"$1a\",\"$1b\",\"$1c\",\"$1d\",\"$1e\",\"$1f\"],\"SaveArticle\"]\n49:I[795595,[\"$10\",\"$11\",\"$12\",\"$13\",\"$14\",\"$18\",\"$19\",\"$1a\",\"$1b\",\"$1c\",\"$1d\",\"$1e\",\"$1f\"],\"ChatNow\"]\n3a:[\"$\",\"$L44\",null,{\"slug\":\"head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\"}]\n3b:[\"$\",\"$L46\",null,{\"slug\":\"head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\",\"careType\":\"$undefined\"}]\n3c:[\"$\",\"$L47\",null,{\"slug\":\"head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\",\"careType\":\"$undefined\",\"title\":\"Head Injury: Warning Signs That Mean Go to the ER Now\"}]\n3d:[\"$\",\"nav\",null,{\"className\":\"eyebrow measure mx-auto mb-3\",\"aria-label\":\"Breadcrumb\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/health\",\"className\":\"text-ink-muted hover:text-blue\",\"children\":\"Health library\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"aria-hidden\":true,\"className\":\"mx-1.5 select-none text-ink-faint\",\"children\":\"/\"}],[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/health/topics/crisis\",\"className\":\"text-ink-muted hover:text-blue\",\"children\":\"Urgent \u0026 emergency\"}]]}]\n3e:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto mb-3\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"inline-block rounded-[50px] px-2 py-0.5 text-[0.6875rem] font-semibold uppercase leading-[1.55] tracking-[0.07em] text-black\",\"style\":{\"background\":\"var(--color-pill-blue)\"},\"children\":\"Urgent \u0026 emergency\"}]}]\n3f:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto mb-4 flex flex-wrap items-start justify-between gap-3\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h1\",null,{\"className\":\"t-display text-ink\",\"children\":\"Head Injury: Warning Signs That Mean Go to the ER Now\"}],[\"$\",\"$L48\",null,{\"slug\":\"head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\",\"title\":\"Head Injury: Warning Signs That Mean Go to the ER Now\",\"domain\":\"crisis\",\"careType\":\"$undefined\"}]]}]\n40:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto mb-4 t-subtitle text-ink\",\"children\":\"Go to the ER after a head injury if the person is unconscious, confused, vomiting repeatedly, having a seizure, has unequal pupils, or cannot walk or speak normally. People taking blood thinners and adults over 65 need emergency evaluation even after a minor blow. A CT scan is the standard test to detect bleeding the symptoms alone cannot rule out.\"}]\n41:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"eyebrow measure mx-auto mb-10 text-ink-muted\",\"children\":[\"Last updated: \",\"July 2026\",[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"aria-hidden\":true,\"className\":\"mx-1.5 select-none text-ink-faint\",\"children\":\"·\"}],[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/health/head-injury-when-to-go-to-er/history\",\"className\":\"underline hover:text-blue\",\"children\":\"History\"}],false]}]\n42:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mx-auto flex flex-col-reverse gap-8 md:flex-row md:gap-16\",\"style\":{\"maxWidth\":\"76rem\",\"borderBottom\":\"3px solid var(--color-hairline-heavy)\",\"paddingBottom\":\"3.5rem\"},\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"w-full md:min-w-0 md:flex-1\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"className\":\"mb-8\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto\",\"style\":{\"background\":\"var(--color-surface)\",\"border\":\"1.5px solid var(--color-ink)\",\"borderRadius\":0,\"boxShadow\":\"3px 3px 0 0 var(--color-ink)\",\"padding\":\"20px\"},\"children\":[[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"eyebrow mb-2 text-neg\",\"children\":\"If this is happening now\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"mb-2 t-body text-ink\",\"children\":\"Call 911 if the person is unconscious, seizing, or cannot be woken. Go to the ER immediately for any red flag above. For mild symptoms with no red flags, urgent care or a primary care visit within 24 hours is appropriate — but monitor closely at home and go to the ER if any new symptoms develop.\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"t-small text-ink-muted\",\"children\":\"Care like this may require 911 or emergency services. Gale doesn't schedule appointments for emergencies — emergency care comes first; scheduled follow-up can wait until you're safe.\"}]]}]}],false,false,[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"t-prose\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"section\",\"0\",{\"id\":\"signs-that-mean-go-to-the-er-immediately\",\"className\":\"scroll-mt-[110px]\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":\"Signs that mean go to the ER immediately\"}],[\"$\",\"$L49\",null,{\"sectionHeading\":\"Signs that mean go to the ER immediately\",\"sectionText\":\"Go immediately — do not wait to see if symptoms improve — if any of these are present after a head injury:\\n\\n- Loss of consciousness, even briefly\\n- Confusion, disorientation, or unusual behavior\\n- Cannot remember the injury or events around it\\n- Repeated vomiting (more than once or twice)\\n- Seizure after the injury\\n- One pupil larger than the other\\n- Severe or worsening headache\\n- Slurred speech, difficulty finding words\\n- Weakness or numbness in an arm or leg\\n- Difficulty walking or balance problems\\n- Visible skull deformity, deep laceration, or [heavy bleeding](/health/bleeding-wont-stop-deep-wound-emergency)\\n- Clear fluid draining from the nose or ears\\n- In infants: will not wake, won’t stop crying, or has a bulging fontanelle (soft spot) [1][2]\",\"articleSlug\":\"head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\",\"articleTitle\":\"Head Injury: Warning Signs That Mean Go to the ER Now\",\"articleDomain\":\"crisis\",\"careType\":\"$undefined\"}],[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":[\"Go immediately — do not wait to see if symptoms improve — if any of these are present after a head injury:\"]}],[\"$\",\"ul\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto list-disc pl-5 text-ink\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"li\",\"0\",{\"children\":[\"Loss of consciousness, even briefly\"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"1\",{\"children\":[\"Confusion, disorientation, or unusual behavior\"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"2\",{\"children\":[\"Cannot remember the injury or events around it\"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"3\",{\"children\":[\"Repeated vomiting (more than once or twice)\"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"4\",{\"children\":[\"Seizure after the injury\"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"5\",{\"children\":[\"One pupil larger than the other\"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"6\",{\"children\":[\"Severe or worsening headache\"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"7\",{\"children\":[\"Slurred speech, difficulty finding words\"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"8\",{\"children\":[\"Weakness or numbness in an arm or leg\"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"9\",{\"children\":[\"Difficulty walking or balance problems\"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"10\",{\"children\":[\"Visible skull deformity, deep laceration, or \",[\"$\",\"a\",\"0\",{\"href\":\"/health/bleeding-wont-stop-deep-wound-emergency\",\"className\":\"font-bold text-ink no-underline border-b-2 border-blue hover:text-blue-deep\",\"children\":[\"heavy bleeding\"]}]]}],\"$L4a\",\"$L4b\"]}]]]}],\"$L4c\",\"$L4d\",\"$L4e\",\"$L4f\"]}],\"$L50\",\"$L51\",\"$L52\",false,false,false,false,\"$L53\",\"$L54\",\"$L55\",\"$L56\"]}],\"$L57\"]}]\n43:[\"$\",\"footer\",null,{\"className\":\"rule-t\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mx-auto flex max-w-6xl flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 px-6 py-6 text-sm text-ink-muted\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":\"Gale — a health publication and health software.\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/stories\",\"children\":\"Stories\"}],[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/for-patients\",\"children\":\"for Patients\"}],[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/providers/claim?src=site-footer\",\"children\":\"Correct or remove a listing\"}],[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/free\",\"children\":\"How Gale is free\"}],[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/why\",\"children\":\"For clinicians - beta\"}],[[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/legal/privacy-policy\",\"children\":\"Privacy\"}],[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/legal/terms-of-service\",\"children\":\"Terms\"}],[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/legal/consumer-health-data-privacy-policy\",\"children\":\"Consumer Health Data Privacy\"}],[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/legal/fee-schedule\",\"children\":\"Fees\"}],[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/sms\",\"children\":\"Text messages\"}],[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/legal\",\"children\":\"Legal\"}]]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pb-6\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"t-micro text-ink-faint\",\"style\":{\"maxWidth\":\"80ch\"},\"children\":\"Gale is the health-technology company at gale.care, operated by Gale Care Inc — a consumer health front door and practice software for independent clinicians. It is not a healthcare staffing agency, and it is not affiliated with Gale Healthcare Solutions, a separate nurse-staffing company at gale-healthcare.com.\"}]}]]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"59:I[498244,[\"$10\",\"$11\",\"$12\",\"$13\",\"$14\",\"$18\",\"$19\",\"$1a\",\"$1b\",\"$1c\",\"$1d\",\"$1e\",\"$1f\"],\"Faq\"]\n5a:I[8174,[\"$10\",\"$11\",\"$12\",\"$13\",\"$14\",\"$18\",\"$19\",\"$1a\",\"$1b\",\"$1c\",\"$1d\",\"$1e\",\"$1f\"],\"TakeToAI\"]\n5b:I[436900,[\"$10\",\"$11\",\"$12\",\"$13\",\"$14\",\"$18\",\"$19\",\"$1a\",\"$1b\",\"$1c\",\"$1d\",\"$1e\",\"$1f\"],\"ArticleRated\"]\n5d:I[679047,[\"$10\",\"$11\",\"$12\",\"$13\",\"$14\",\"$18\",\"$19\",\"$1a\",\"$1b\",\"$1c\",\"$1d\",\"$1e\",\"$1f\"],\"Toc\"]\n4a:[\"$\",\"li\",\"11\",{\"children\":[\"Clear fluid draining from the nose or ears\"]}]\n4b:[\"$\",\"li\",\"12\",{\"children\":[\"In infants: will not wake, won’t stop crying, or has a bulging fontanelle (soft spot) \",[\"$\",\"span\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"group relative inline\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"#ref-1\",\"className\":\"nums ml-px align-super text-[0.64em] font-semibold text-violet decoration-transparent hover:text-violet-deep focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-violet/40\",\"children\":1}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"role\":\"note\",\"className\":\"pointer-events-none invisible absolute bottom-full left-1/2 z-40 mb-1.5 w-72 -translate-x-1/2 whitespace-normal border border-hairline bg-surface p-3 text-left opacity-0 shadow-sm transition-opacity group-hover:visible group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-within:visible group-focus-within:opacity-100\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"block font-mono text-[0.62rem] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-faint\",\"children\":[\"Ref \",1]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block text-[0.78rem] leading-snug text-ink-muted\",\"children\":[\"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\",\" (\",2025,\").\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block font-serif text-[0.9rem] leading-snug text-ink\",\"children\":[\"Signs and Symptoms of Concussion\",\".\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-1 block text-[0.74rem] leading-snug text-ink-muted\",\"children\":\"Concussion symptom clusters including headache, fogginess, sensitivity to light, nausea, dizziness, and the danger signs that require emergency care (seizure, unequal pupils, repeated vomiting, loss of consciousness)\"}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"span\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"group relative inline\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"#ref-2\",\"className\":\"nums ml-px align-super text-[0.64em] font-semibold text-violet decoration-transparent hover:text-violet-deep focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-violet/40\",\"children\":2}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"role\":\"note\",\"className\":\"pointer-events-none invisible absolute bottom-full left-1/2 z-40 mb-1.5 w-72 -translate-x-1/2 whitespace-normal border border-hairline bg-surface p-3 text-left opacity-0 shadow-sm transition-opacity group-hover:visible group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-within:visible group-focus-within:opacity-100\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"block font-mono text-[0.62rem] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-faint\",\"children\":[\"Ref \",2]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block text-[0.78rem] leading-snug text-ink-muted\",\"children\":[\"American Academy of Pediatrics\",\" (\",2024,\").\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block font-serif text-[0.9rem] leading-snug text-ink\",\"children\":[\"Head Injury in Children: How to Know If It's Minor or Serious\",\".\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-1 block text-[0.74rem] leading-snug text-ink-muted\",\"children\":\"Pediatric head-injury red flags warranting ER evaluation: loss of consciousness, persistent headache, vomiting more than 2–3 times, difficulty waking, unequal pupils, seizures, and limb weakness; guidance on home observation after minor bump\"}]]}]]}]]}]\n4c:[\"$\",\"section\",\"1\",{\"id\":\"what-is-a-concussion-and-why-does-it-need-evaluation\",\"className\":\"scroll-mt-[110px]\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":\"What is a concussion — and why does it need evaluation?\"}],[\"$\",\"$L49\",null,{\"sectionHeading\":\"What is a concussion — and why does it need evaluation?\",\"sectionText\":\"A concussion is a brain injury caused by the head being shaken or struck. It does not require a loss of consciousness — someone can be concussed and walk away or seem fine for an hour before symptoms develop. The CDC’s HEADS UP program [1] identifies four symptom domains: physical (headache, dizziness, nausea, sensitivity to light or noise), cognitive (fogginess, difficulty concentrating, memory gaps), emotional (irritability, anxiety, sadness), and sleep (sleeping more or less than usual, insomnia).\\n\\nConcussions are real brain injuries. Most resolve with rest, but some people — especially those who return to activity too soon or have had prior concussions — take longer to recover or develop lasting problems. Any suspected concussion deserves a clinician’s evaluation.\",\"articleSlug\":\"head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\",\"articleTitle\":\"Head Injury: Warning Signs That Mean Go to the ER Now\",\"articleDomain\":\"crisis\",\"careType\":\"$undefined\"}],[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":[\"A concussion is a brain injury caused by the head being shaken or struck. It does not require a loss of consciousness — someone can be concussed and walk away or seem fine for an hour before symptoms develop. The CDC’s HEADS UP program \",[\"$\",\"span\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"group relative inline\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"#ref-1\",\"className\":\"nums ml-px align-super text-[0.64em] font-semibold text-violet decoration-transparent hover:text-violet-deep focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-violet/40\",\"children\":1}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"role\":\"note\",\"className\":\"pointer-events-none invisible absolute bottom-full left-1/2 z-40 mb-1.5 w-72 -translate-x-1/2 whitespace-normal border border-hairline bg-surface p-3 text-left opacity-0 shadow-sm transition-opacity group-hover:visible group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-within:visible group-focus-within:opacity-100\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"block font-mono text-[0.62rem] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-faint\",\"children\":[\"Ref \",1]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block text-[0.78rem] leading-snug text-ink-muted\",\"children\":[\"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\",\" (\",2025,\").\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block font-serif text-[0.9rem] leading-snug text-ink\",\"children\":[\"Signs and Symptoms of Concussion\",\".\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-1 block text-[0.74rem] leading-snug text-ink-muted\",\"children\":\"Concussion symptom clusters including headache, fogginess, sensitivity to light, nausea, dizziness, and the danger signs that require emergency care (seizure, unequal pupils, repeated vomiting, loss of consciousness)\"}]]}]]}],\" identifies four symptom domains: physical (headache, dizziness, nausea, sensitivity to light or noise), cognitive (fogginess, difficulty concentrating, memory gaps), emotional (irritability, anxiety, sadness), and sleep (sleeping more or less than usual, insomnia).\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":[\"Concussions are real brain injuries. Most resolve with rest, but some people — especially those who return to activity too soon or have had prior concussions — take longer to recover or develop lasting problems. Any suspected concussion deserves a clinician’s evaluation.\"]}]]]}]\n4d:[\"$\",\"section\",\"2\",{\"id\":\"who-needs-extra-caution-after-a-head-injury\",\"className\":\"scroll-mt-[110px]\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":\"Who needs extra caution after a head injury\"}],[\"$\",\"$L49\",null,{\"sectionHeading\":\"Who needs extra caution after a head injury\",\"sectionText\":\"**People on blood thinners:** Anticoagulant medications — warfarin, apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran — and higher-dose aspirin prevent clotting and raise the risk of bleeding in and around the brain after even a modest impact. Any head injury in this group warrants emergency evaluation.\\n\\n**Adults over 65:** The brain changes with age, leaving more room where blood can accumulate before pressure becomes obvious. Older adults can develop slow bleeds — subdural hematomas — that cause subtle symptoms days to weeks after an injury.\\n\\n**Infants under 1 year:** Infants cannot describe symptoms. Any significant head injury in an infant warrants emergency evaluation — do not wait for obvious symptoms to appear [2].\\n\\n**Anyone with prior concussions:** A history of head injuries can increase recovery time and, in some situations, raise the risk of complications with subsequent impacts.\",\"articleSlug\":\"head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\",\"articleTitle\":\"Head Injury: Warning Signs That Mean Go to the ER Now\",\"articleDomain\":\"crisis\",\"careType\":\"$undefined\"}],[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",\"0\",{\"children\":[\"People on blood thinners:\"]}],\" Anticoagulant medications — warfarin, apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran — and higher-dose aspirin prevent clotting and raise the risk of bleeding in and around the brain after even a modest impact. Any head injury in this group warrants emergency evaluation.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",\"0\",{\"children\":[\"Adults over 65:\"]}],\" The brain changes with age, leaving more room where blood can accumulate before pressure becomes obvious. Older adults can develop slow bleeds — subdural hematomas — that cause subtle symptoms days to weeks after an injury.\"]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"2\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",\"0\",{\"children\":[\"Infants under 1 year:\"]}],\" Infants cannot describe symptoms. Any significant head injury in an infant warrants emergency evaluation — do not wait for obvious symptoms to appear \",[\"$\",\"span\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"group relative inline\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"#ref-2\",\"className\":\"nums ml-px align-super text-[0.64em] font-semibold text-violet decoration-transparent hover:text-violet-deep focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-violet/40\",\"children\":2}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"role\":\"note\",\"className\":\"pointer-events-none invisible absolute bottom-full left-1/2 z-40 mb-1.5 w-72 -translate-x-1/2 whitespace-normal border border-hairline bg-surface p-3 text-left opacity-0 shadow-sm transition-opacity group-hover:visible group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-within:visible group-focus-within:opacity-100\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"block font-mono text-[0.62rem] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-faint\",\"children\":[\"Ref \",2]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block text-[0.78rem] leading-snug text-ink-muted\",\"children\":[\"American Academy of Pediatrics\",\" (\",2024,\").\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block font-serif text-[0.9rem] leading-snug text-ink\",\"children\":[\"Head Injury in Children: How to Know If It's Minor or Serious\",\".\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-1 block text-[0.74rem] leading-snug text-ink-muted\",\"children\":\"Pediatric head-injury red flags warranting ER evaluation: loss of consciousness, persistent headache, vomiting more than 2–3 times, difficulty waking, unequal pupils, seizures, and limb weakness; guidance on home observation after minor bump\"}]]}]]}],\".\"]}],\"$L58\"]]}]\n4e:[\"$\",\"section\",\"3\",{\"id\":\"after-a-minor-bump-what-to-watch-for-at-home\",\"className\":\"scroll-mt-[110px]\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":\"After a minor bump: what to watch for at home\"}],[\"$\",\"$L49\",null,{\"sectionHeading\":\"After a minor bump: what to watch for at home\",\"sectionText\":\"If none of the emergency signs above are present after a minor bump to the head, careful home observation for 24 hours is appropriate. The American Academy of Pediatrics [2] recommends checking the child every 2–3 hours to confirm normal movement, responsiveness, and recognition. Watch for:\\n\\n- [Worsening headache](/health/sudden-worst-headache-of-my-life-emergency)\\n- New vomiting\\n- Increasing confusion or unusual behavior\\n- Difficulty waking up or staying awake\\n- New weakness, numbness, or coordination problems\\n- A seizure\\n\\nIf any of these develop, go to the ER immediately — do not wait until morning.\",\"articleSlug\":\"head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\",\"articleTitle\":\"Head Injury: Warning Signs That Mean Go to the ER Now\",\"articleDomain\":\"crisis\",\"careType\":\"$undefined\"}],[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":[\"If none of the emergency signs above are present after a minor bump to the head, careful home observation for 24 hours is appropriate. The American Academy of Pediatrics \",[\"$\",\"span\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"group relative inline\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"#ref-2\",\"className\":\"nums ml-px align-super text-[0.64em] font-semibold text-violet decoration-transparent hover:text-violet-deep focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-violet/40\",\"children\":2}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"role\":\"note\",\"className\":\"pointer-events-none invisible absolute bottom-full left-1/2 z-40 mb-1.5 w-72 -translate-x-1/2 whitespace-normal border border-hairline bg-surface p-3 text-left opacity-0 shadow-sm transition-opacity group-hover:visible group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-within:visible group-focus-within:opacity-100\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"block font-mono text-[0.62rem] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-faint\",\"children\":[\"Ref \",2]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block text-[0.78rem] leading-snug text-ink-muted\",\"children\":[\"American Academy of Pediatrics\",\" (\",2024,\").\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block font-serif text-[0.9rem] leading-snug text-ink\",\"children\":[\"Head Injury in Children: How to Know If It's Minor or Serious\",\".\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-1 block text-[0.74rem] leading-snug text-ink-muted\",\"children\":\"Pediatric head-injury red flags warranting ER evaluation: loss of consciousness, persistent headache, vomiting more than 2–3 times, difficulty waking, unequal pupils, seizures, and limb weakness; guidance on home observation after minor bump\"}]]}]]}],\" recommends checking the child every 2–3 hours to confirm normal movement, responsiveness, and recognition. Watch for:\"]}],[\"$\",\"ul\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto list-disc pl-5 text-ink\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"li\",\"0\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"a\",\"0\",{\"href\":\"/health/sudden-worst-headache-of-my-life-emergency\",\"className\":\"font-bold text-ink no-underline border-b-2 border-blue hover:text-blue-deep\",\"children\":[\"Worsening headache\"]}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"1\",{\"children\":[\"New vomiting\"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"2\",{\"children\":[\"Increasing confusion or unusual behavior\"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"3\",{\"children\":[\"Difficulty waking up or staying awake\"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"4\",{\"children\":[\"New weakness, numbness, or coordination problems\"]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"5\",{\"children\":[\"A seizure\"]}]]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"2\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":[\"If any of these develop, go to the ER immediately — do not wait until morning.\"]}]]]}]\n4f:[\"$\",\"section\",\"4\",{\"id\":\"what-a-clinician-will-do\",\"className\":\"scroll-mt-[110px]\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":\"What a clinician will do\"}],[\"$\",\"$L49\",null,{\"sectionHeading\":\"What a clinician will do\",\"sectionText\":\"A clinician will ask how the injury happened, whether consciousness was lost and for how long, what symptoms have appeared, and whether blood thinners are involved. CT scan is the first-line imaging modality in the ER for suspected intracranial injury — it can rapidly detect bleeding, fractures, and swelling [3]. Clinical decision rules (such as the NEXUS Head criteria or Canadian CT Head Rule) help guide selective CT use in mild head trauma, avoiding unnecessary radiation while ensuring serious injuries are not missed. For concussion without red flags, standardized assessment tools help grade symptom severity and guide return-to-activity decisions.\",\"articleSlug\":\"head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\",\"articleTitle\":\"Head Injury: Warning Signs That Mean Go to the ER Now\",\"articleDomain\":\"crisis\",\"careType\":\"$undefined\"}],[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":[\"A clinician will ask how the injury happened, whether consciousness was lost and for how long, what symptoms have appeared, and whether blood thinners are involved. CT scan is the first-line imaging modality in the ER for suspected intracranial injury — it can rapidly detect bleeding, fractures, and swelling \",[\"$\",\"span\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"group relative inline\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"#ref-3\",\"className\":\"nums ml-px align-super text-[0.64em] font-semibold text-violet decoration-transparent hover:text-violet-deep focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-violet/40\",\"children\":3}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"role\":\"note\",\"className\":\"pointer-events-none invisible absolute bottom-full left-1/2 z-40 mb-1.5 w-72 -translate-x-1/2 whitespace-normal border border-hairline bg-surface p-3 text-left opacity-0 shadow-sm transition-opacity group-hover:visible group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-within:visible group-focus-within:opacity-100\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"block font-mono text-[0.62rem] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-faint\",\"children\":[\"Ref \",3]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block text-[0.78rem] leading-snug text-ink-muted\",\"children\":[\"Expert Panel on Neurological Imaging; Shih RY, Burns J, et al.\",\" (\",2021,\").\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block font-serif text-[0.9rem] leading-snug text-ink\",\"children\":[\"ACR Appropriateness Criteria® Head Trauma: 2021 Update\",\".\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-1 block text-[0.74rem] leading-snug text-ink-muted\",\"children\":\"CT as the first-line imaging modality for suspected intracranial injury; selective CT use in mild head trauma based on clinical decision rules; role of CT in detecting bleeding, fractures, and swelling\"}]]}]]}],\". Clinical decision rules (such as the NEXUS Head criteria or Canadian CT Head Rule) help guide selective CT use in mild head trauma, avoiding unnecessary radiation while ensuring serious injuries are not missed. For concussion without red flags, standardized assessment tools help grade symptom severity and guide return-to-activity decisions.\"]}]]]}]\n50:[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-12\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"eyebrow measure mx-auto mb-4\",\"children\":\"Common questions\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L59\",null,{\"items\":[{\"q\":\"Can I have a serious head injury without losing consciousness?\",\"a\":[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":[\"Yes. A concussion does not require loss of consciousness. Someone can feel fine immediately after a head impact and develop symptoms — headache, fogginess, nausea — minutes to hours later. If any of the red flag symptoms appear after a head injury, go to the ER regardless of whether consciousness was lost.\"]}]]},{\"q\":\"Is it safe to sleep after a head injury?\",\"a\":[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":[\"For most minor bumps with no red flag symptoms, sleep is safe. The old advice to keep someone awake all night is not current guidance. However, it is reasonable to check on the person periodically to make sure they can be roused and are not confused. If they become very difficult to wake or develop new symptoms, go to the ER immediately.\"]}]]},{\"q\":\"Why are blood thinners a special concern after a head injury?\",\"a\":[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":[\"Blood thinners reduce the blood's ability to clot. After a head injury, a small bleed that a healthy person's body would stop can continue to expand in someone on anticoagulants. This can cause pressure on the brain even from an injury that seemed minor. Any head injury in someone on warfarin, apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, or higher-dose aspirin warrants emergency evaluation.\"]}]]},{\"q\":\"When can someone with a concussion return to sport or activity?\",\"a\":[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":[\"Return to sport or strenuous activity after a concussion should follow a stepwise protocol guided by a clinician, with each stage completed symptom-free before advancing. Returning too quickly — while still having any symptoms — raises the risk of a second injury before the brain has healed. A sports medicine or primary care clinician can help build this plan.\"]}]]}]}]}]]}]\n51:[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-12\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"eyebrow measure mx-auto mb-4\",\"children\":\"Related\"}],[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto flex flex-col gap-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"li\",\"high-fever-stiff-neck-rash-child-meningitis\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"eyebrow mb-1 text-ink-muted\",\"children\":\"Urgent \u0026 emergency\"}],[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/health/high-fever-stiff-neck-rash-child-meningitis\",\"className\":\"t-card-title text-ink hover:text-blue-deep\",\"children\":\"High Fever, Stiff Neck, and Rash in a Child: Go to the ER Now\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"how-to-stop-a-nosebleed-that-wont-stop\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"eyebrow mb-1 text-ink-muted\",\"children\":\"Urgent \u0026 emergency\"}],[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/health/how-to-stop-a-nosebleed-that-wont-stop\",\"className\":\"t-card-title text-ink hover:text-blue-deep\",\"children\":\"Nosebleed That Won't Stop: How to Control It and When to Go to the ER\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"when-a-teen-s-hopelessness-becomes-a-mental-health-emergency\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"eyebrow mb-1 text-ink-muted\",\"children\":\"Urgent \u0026 emergency\"}],[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/health/when-a-teen-s-hopelessness-becomes-a-mental-health-emergency\",\"className\":\"t-card-title text-ink hover:text-blue-deep\",\"children\":\"When a Teen's Hopelessness Becomes a Mental Health Emergency\"}]]}]]}]]}]\n52:null\n53:[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"id\":\"safety\",\"className\":\"mt-12\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto\",\"style\":{\"background\":\"var(--color-surface)\",\"border\":\"1.5px solid var(--color-ink)\",\"borderRadius\":0,\"boxShadow\":\"3px 3px 0 0 var(--color-ink)\",\"padding\":\"24px\"},\"children\":[[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"eyebrow mb-3 text-neg\",\"children\":\"Call 911 or go to the ER now for any of these\"}],[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"className\":\"mb-3 flex flex-col gap-1.5 t-small text-ink\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"li\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"flex gap-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"select-none text-neg\",\"children\":\"—\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":\"Loss of consciousness, even briefly\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"flex gap-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"select-none text-neg\",\"children\":\"—\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":\"Cannot be woken up or is very difficult to rouse\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"2\",{\"className\":\"flex gap-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"select-none text-neg\",\"children\":\"—\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":\"Repeated vomiting after the injury\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"3\",{\"className\":\"flex gap-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"select-none text-neg\",\"children\":\"—\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":\"Seizure\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"4\",{\"className\":\"flex gap-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"select-none text-neg\",\"children\":\"—\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":\"Unequal pupils\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"5\",{\"className\":\"flex gap-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"select-none text-neg\",\"children\":\"—\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":\"Severe headache or headache that is rapidly worsening\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"6\",{\"className\":\"flex gap-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"select-none text-neg\",\"children\":\"—\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":\"Slurred speech, confusion, or inability to recognize people or places\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"7\",{\"className\":\"flex gap-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"select-none text-neg\",\"children\":\"—\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":\"Weakness, numbness, or trouble walking\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"8\",{\"className\":\"flex gap-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"select-none text-neg\",\"children\":\"—\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":\"Clear fluid draining from the nose or ears\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"9\",{\"className\":\"flex gap-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"select-none text-neg\",\"children\":\"—\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":\"Visible skull deformity\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"10\",{\"className\":\"flex gap-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"select-none text-neg\",\"children\":\"—\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":\"Infant who will not stop crying or has a bulging soft spot\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"11\",{\"className\":\"flex gap-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"select-none text-neg\",\"children\":\"—\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":\"Person on blood thinners — any head injury\"}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"mb-3 t-small font-semibold text-ink\",\"children\":\"Call 911 if the person is unconscious, seizing, or cannot be woken. Go to the ER immediately for any red flag above. For mild symptoms with no red flags, urgent care or a primary care visit within 24 hours is appropriate — but monitor closely at home and go to the ER if any new symptoms develop.\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"t-micro text-ink-muted\",\"children\":\"This article is general health information and not a diagnosis. If you or someone with you has any of the signs above after a head injury, call 911 or go to the emergency room immediately.\"}]]}]}]\n54:[\"$\",\"$L5a\",null,{\"article\":{\"slug\":\"head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\",\"id\":\"hs-0166\",\"query\":\"head injury when to go to the er\",\"h1\":\"Head Injury: Warning Signs That Mean Go to the ER Now\",\"domain\":\"crisis\",\"intent\":\"emergency\",\"careType\":null,\"emergency\":true,\"meta\":{\"title\":\"Head Injury: When to Go to the ER — Key Warning Signs\",\"description\":\"Confusion, repeated vomiting, seizures, or unequal pupils after a head injury mean go to the ER now. Know the red flags, plus risks for blood thinner users.\",\"keywords\":[\"head injury when to go to the ER\",\"concussion warning signs\",\"hit my head when should I worry\",\"head trauma emergency symptoms\",\"signs of brain bleed after hitting head\",\"concussion symptoms in adults\",\"head injury on blood thinners\",\"head injury red flags\",\"when to get a CT scan after head injury\",\"elderly fall head injury what to do\"],\"schemaType\":\"MedicalWebPage\"},\"aboveTheFold\":\"Call 911 or go to the emergency room right now if the person is unconscious, confused, vomiting repeatedly, having a seizure, has unequal pupils, or cannot walk or speak normally after a head injury [1]. People on blood thinners and adults over 65 need a lower threshold for emergency care — even a modest blow can cause significant bleeding. When in doubt, go. A CT scan can detect bleeding that symptoms alone cannot rule out.\",\"snippet\":\"Go to the ER after a head injury if the person is unconscious, confused, vomiting repeatedly, having a seizure, has unequal pupils, or cannot walk or speak normally. People taking blood thinners and adults over 65 need emergency evaluation even after a minor blow. A CT scan is the standard test to detect bleeding the symptoms alone cannot rule out.\",\"body\":[{\"heading\":\"Signs that mean go to the ER immediately\",\"level\":\"h2\",\"markdown\":\"Go immediately — do not wait to see if symptoms improve — if any of these are present after a head injury:\\n\\n- Loss of consciousness, even briefly\\n- Confusion, disorientation, or unusual behavior\\n- Cannot remember the injury or events around it\\n- Repeated vomiting (more than once or twice)\\n- Seizure after the injury\\n- One pupil larger than the other\\n- Severe or worsening headache\\n- Slurred speech, difficulty finding words\\n- Weakness or numbness in an arm or leg\\n- Difficulty walking or balance problems\\n- Visible skull deformity, deep laceration, or [heavy bleeding](/health/bleeding-wont-stop-deep-wound-emergency)\\n- Clear fluid draining from the nose or ears\\n- In infants: will not wake, won’t stop crying, or has a bulging fontanelle (soft spot) [1][2]\"},{\"heading\":\"What is a concussion — and why does it need evaluation?\",\"level\":\"h2\",\"markdown\":\"A concussion is a brain injury caused by the head being shaken or struck. It does not require a loss of consciousness — someone can be concussed and walk away or seem fine for an hour before symptoms develop. The CDC’s HEADS UP program [1] identifies four symptom domains: physical (headache, dizziness, nausea, sensitivity to light or noise), cognitive (fogginess, difficulty concentrating, memory gaps), emotional (irritability, anxiety, sadness), and sleep (sleeping more or less than usual, insomnia).\\n\\nConcussions are real brain injuries. Most resolve with rest, but some people — especially those who return to activity too soon or have had prior concussions — take longer to recover or develop lasting problems. Any suspected concussion deserves a clinician’s evaluation.\"},{\"heading\":\"Who needs extra caution after a head injury\",\"level\":\"h2\",\"markdown\":\"**People on blood thinners:** Anticoagulant medications — warfarin, apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran — and higher-dose aspirin prevent clotting and raise the risk of bleeding in and around the brain after even a modest impact. Any head injury in this group warrants emergency evaluation.\\n\\n**Adults over 65:** The brain changes with age, leaving more room where blood can accumulate before pressure becomes obvious. Older adults can develop slow bleeds — subdural hematomas — that cause subtle symptoms days to weeks after an injury.\\n\\n**Infants under 1 year:** Infants cannot describe symptoms. Any significant head injury in an infant warrants emergency evaluation — do not wait for obvious symptoms to appear [2].\\n\\n**Anyone with prior concussions:** A history of head injuries can increase recovery time and, in some situations, raise the risk of complications with subsequent impacts.\"},{\"heading\":\"After a minor bump: what to watch for at home\",\"level\":\"h2\",\"markdown\":\"If none of the emergency signs above are present after a minor bump to the head, careful home observation for 24 hours is appropriate. The American Academy of Pediatrics [2] recommends checking the child every 2–3 hours to confirm normal movement, responsiveness, and recognition. Watch for:\\n\\n- [Worsening headache](/health/sudden-worst-headache-of-my-life-emergency)\\n- New vomiting\\n- Increasing confusion or unusual behavior\\n- Difficulty waking up or staying awake\\n- New weakness, numbness, or coordination problems\\n- A seizure\\n\\nIf any of these develop, go to the ER immediately — do not wait until morning.\"},{\"heading\":\"What a clinician will do\",\"level\":\"h2\",\"markdown\":\"A clinician will ask how the injury happened, whether consciousness was lost and for how long, what symptoms have appeared, and whether blood thinners are involved. CT scan is the first-line imaging modality in the ER for suspected intracranial injury — it can rapidly detect bleeding, fractures, and swelling [3]. Clinical decision rules (such as the NEXUS Head criteria or Canadian CT Head Rule) help guide selective CT use in mild head trauma, avoiding unnecessary radiation while ensuring serious injuries are not missed. For concussion without red flags, standardized assessment tools help grade symptom severity and guide return-to-activity decisions.\"}],\"faq\":[{\"q\":\"Can I have a serious head injury without losing consciousness?\",\"a\":\"Yes. A concussion does not require loss of consciousness. Someone can feel fine immediately after a head impact and develop symptoms — headache, fogginess, nausea — minutes to hours later. If any of the red flag symptoms appear after a head injury, go to the ER regardless of whether consciousness was lost.\"},{\"q\":\"Is it safe to sleep after a head injury?\",\"a\":\"For most minor bumps with no red flag symptoms, sleep is safe. The old advice to keep someone awake all night is not current guidance. However, it is reasonable to check on the person periodically to make sure they can be roused and are not confused. If they become very difficult to wake or develop new symptoms, go to the ER immediately.\"},{\"q\":\"Why are blood thinners a special concern after a head injury?\",\"a\":\"Blood thinners reduce the blood's ability to clot. After a head injury, a small bleed that a healthy person's body would stop can continue to expand in someone on anticoagulants. This can cause pressure on the brain even from an injury that seemed minor. Any head injury in someone on warfarin, apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, or higher-dose aspirin warrants emergency evaluation.\"},{\"q\":\"When can someone with a concussion return to sport or activity?\",\"a\":\"Return to sport or strenuous activity after a concussion should follow a stepwise protocol guided by a clinician, with each stage completed symptom-free before advancing. Returning too quickly — while still having any symptoms — raises the risk of a second injury before the brain has healed. A sports medicine or primary care clinician can help build this plan.\"}],\"safetyBox\":{\"title\":\"Call 911 or go to the ER now for any of these\",\"redFlags\":[\"Loss of consciousness, even briefly\",\"Cannot be woken up or is very difficult to rouse\",\"Repeated vomiting after the injury\",\"Seizure\",\"Unequal pupils\",\"Severe headache or headache that is rapidly worsening\",\"Slurred speech, confusion, or inability to recognize people or places\",\"Weakness, numbness, or trouble walking\",\"Clear fluid draining from the nose or ears\",\"Visible skull deformity\",\"Infant who will not stop crying or has a bulging soft spot\",\"Person on blood thinners — any head injury\"],\"emergencyRouting\":\"Call 911 if the person is unconscious, seizing, or cannot be woken. Go to the ER immediately for any red flag above. For mild symptoms with no red flags, urgent care or a primary care visit within 24 hours is appropriate — but monitor closely at home and go to the ER if any new symptoms develop.\",\"disclaimer\":\"This article is general health information and not a diagnosis. If you or someone with you has any of the signs above after a head injury, call 911 or go to the emergency room immediately.\"},\"citations\":[{\"n\":1,\"id\":\"cdc-heads-up-signs-symptoms-2025\",\"authors\":\"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\",\"year\":2025,\"title\":\"Signs and Symptoms of Concussion\",\"venue\":\"CDC HEADS UP Program\",\"doi\":\"\",\"pmid\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://www.cdc.gov/heads-up/signs-symptoms/index.html\",\"supports\":\"Concussion symptom clusters including headache, fogginess, sensitivity to light, nausea, dizziness, and the danger signs that require emergency care (seizure, unequal pupils, repeated vomiting, loss of consciousness)\",\"verified\":true},{\"n\":2,\"id\":\"aap-head-injury-children-2024\",\"authors\":\"American Academy of Pediatrics\",\"year\":2024,\"title\":\"Head Injury in Children: How to Know If It's Minor or Serious\",\"venue\":\"HealthyChildren.org\",\"doi\":\"\",\"pmid\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://www.healthychildren.org/English/health-issues/injuries-emergencies/Pages/Head-Injury.aspx\",\"supports\":\"Pediatric head-injury red flags warranting ER evaluation: loss of consciousness, persistent headache, vomiting more than 2–3 times, difficulty waking, unequal pupils, seizures, and limb weakness; guidance on home observation after minor bump\",\"verified\":true},{\"n\":3,\"id\":\"acr-head-trauma-ct-2021\",\"authors\":\"Expert Panel on Neurological Imaging; Shih RY, Burns J, et al.\",\"year\":2021,\"title\":\"ACR Appropriateness Criteria® Head Trauma: 2021 Update\",\"venue\":\"Journal of the American College of Radiology\",\"doi\":\"10.1016/j.jacr.2021.01.006\",\"pmid\":\"33958108\",\"url\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33958108/\",\"supports\":\"CT as the first-line imaging modality for suspected intracranial injury; selective CT use in mild head trauma based on clinical decision rules; role of CT in detecting bleeding, fractures, and swelling\",\"verified\":true}],\"matchedProvider\":null,\"route\":\"library\",\"updated\":\"2026-06-12\",\"modified\":\"2026-07-10\"}}]\n55:[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"id\":\"references\",\"className\":\"mt-12\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto mb-6\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L5b\",null,{\"slug\":\"head-injury-when-to-go-to-er\",\"careType\":\"$undefined\"}]}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"eyebrow measure mx-auto mb-3\",\"children\":\"References\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"ol\",null,{\"className\":\"flex flex-col gap-2.5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"li\",\"1\",{\"id\":\"ref-1\",\"className\":\"scroll-mt-24 flex gap-2.5 t-small leading-snug\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"nums shrink-0 font-semibold text-blue-deep\",\"children\":[1,\".\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"text-ink-muted\",\"children\":[\"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\",\" (\",2025,\"). \",[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"italic\",\"children\":[\"Signs and Symptoms of Concussion\",\".\"]}],\" \",\"CDC HEADS UP Program\",\".\"]}],\" \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.cdc.gov/heads-up/signs-symptoms/index.html\",\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noreferrer\",\"className\":\"break-words font-semibold text-ink no-underline [border-bottom:2px_solid_var(--color-blue)] hover:text-blue-deep\",\"children\":\"link\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"text-pos\",\"title\":\"verified against the source\",\"children\":\" ✓\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block t-micro text-ink-muted\",\"children\":\"Concussion symptom clusters including headache, fogginess, sensitivity to light, nausea, dizziness, and the danger signs that require emergency care (seizure, unequal pupils, repeated vomiting, loss of consciousness)\"}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"2\",{\"id\":\"ref-2\",\"className\":\"scroll-mt-24 flex gap-2.5 t-small leading-snug\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"nums shrink-0 font-semibold text-blue-deep\",\"children\":[2,\".\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"text-ink-muted\",\"children\":[\"American Academy of Pediatrics\",\" (\",2024,\"). \",[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"italic\",\"children\":[\"Head Injury in Children: How to Know If It's Minor or Serious\",\".\"]}],\" \",\"HealthyChildren.org\",\".\"]}],\" \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.healthychildren.org/English/health-issues/injuries-emergencies/Pages/Head-Injury.aspx\",\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noreferrer\",\"className\":\"break-words font-semibold text-ink no-underline [border-bottom:2px_solid_var(--color-blue)] hover:text-blue-deep\",\"children\":\"link\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"text-pos\",\"title\":\"verified against the source\",\"children\":\" ✓\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block t-micro text-ink-muted\",\"children\":\"Pediatric head-injury red flags warranting ER evaluation: loss of consciousness, persistent headache, vomiting more than 2–3 times, difficulty waking, unequal pupils, seizures, and limb weakness; guidance on home observation after minor bump\"}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"li\",\"3\",{\"id\":\"ref-3\",\"className\":\"scroll-mt-24 flex gap-2.5 t-small leading-snug\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"nums shrink-0 font-semibold text-blue-deep\",\"children\":[3,\".\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"text-ink-muted\",\"children\":[\"Expert Panel on Neurological Imaging; Shih RY, Burns J, et al.\",\" (\",2021,\"). \",[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"italic\",\"children\":[\"ACR Appropriateness Criteria® Head Trauma: 2021 Update\",\".\"]}],\" \",\"Journal of the American College of Radiology\",\".\"]}],\" \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33958108/\",\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noreferrer\",\"className\":\"break-words font-semibold text-ink no-underline [border-bottom:2px_solid_var(--color-blue)] hover:text-blue-deep\",\"children\":\"doi:10.1016/j.jacr.2021.01.006\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"text-pos\",\"title\":\"verified against the source\",\"children\":\" ✓\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-0.5 block t-micro text-ink-muted\",\"children\":\"CT as the first-line imaging modality for suspected intracranial injury; selective CT use in mild head trauma based on clinical decision rules; role of CT in detecting bleeding, fractures, and swelling\"}]]}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-6 rule-t pt-4 t-micro text-ink-muted\",\"children\":[3,\" sources, numbered by first appearance. General health information, not medical advice. AI-assisted editorial content\",\" — every citation independently verified\",\". \",\"$L5c\"]}]]}]]}]\n56:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto mt-12\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/health\",\"className\":\"inline-block rounded-none border-[1.5px] border-ink bg-surface px-4 py-3 text-[0.9375rem] font-medium text-ink no-underline [box-shadow:3px_3px_0_0_var(--color-blue)] transition-transform hover:translate-y-[2px] hover:text-ink\",\"children\":\"← Back to the health library\"}]}]\n57:[\"$\",\"aside\",null,{\"className\":\"w-full md:w-auto md:min-w-[300px] md:flex-[0_1_475px]\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L5d\",null,{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"signs-that-mean-go-to-the-er-immediately\",\"title\":\"Signs that mean go to the ER immediately\",\"level\":2},{\"id\":\"what-is-a-concussion-and-why-does-it-need-evaluation\",\"title\":\"What is a concussion — and why does it need evaluation?\",\"level\":2},{\"id\":\"who-needs-extra-caution-after-a-head-injury\",\"title\":\"Who needs extra caution after a head injury\",\"level\":2},{\"id\":\"after-a-minor-bump-what-to-watch-for-at-home\",\"title\":\"After a minor bump: what to watch for at home\",\"level\":2},{\"id\":\"what-a-clinician-will-do\",\"title\":\"What a clinician will do\",\"level\":2}],\"back\":{\"href\":\"/health\",\"label\":\"the health library\"}}],false]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"58:[\"$\",\"p\",\"3\",{\"className\":\"measure mx-auto text-ink\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",\"0\",{\"children\":[\"Anyone with prior concussions:\"]}],\" A history of head injuries can increase recovery time and, in some situations, raise the risk of complications with subsequent impacts.\"]}]\n5c:[\"$\",\"$L34\",null,{\"href\":\"/editorial-policy\",\"className\":\"underline decoration-dotted underline-offset-2\",\"children\":\"Editorial policy\"}]\n"])</script></body></html>